# An interval with one-limb bounds split at its midpoint (milestone2.md, "One enclosure
# interface", the row of split() and "The empty set and the whole line"): [xl, m] and [m, xu], m the
# midpoint of interval_mid_rad_1, which is in [xl, xu]; their union is the interval. The whole line
# gives [-inf, 0] and [0, +inf], a half-line [l, +inf] gives [l, Omega] and [Omega, +inf], and the
# empty set the empty set twice, told by its radius, a NaN.
kernel:        interval_split_1
input:         stored xl
input:         stored xu
output:        al
output:        au
output:        bl
output:        bu
requires:      interval xl xu
ensures:       splits al au bl bu from=xl,xu
min_precision: 2
source:        the bounds and the midpoint of interval_mid_rad_1 (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the midpoint is selected after the test of the radius that tells the empty set; splits states what comes out for every canonical interval

(m, r) = interval_mid_rad_1(xl, xu)
ne     = eq(r, r)
mi     = neg(const inf)
al     = copy(xl)
au     = select(ne, m, mi)
bl     = select(ne, m, const inf)
bu     = copy(xu)
